---
{{ hash_banner() }}
#
# What a planner that interpolates in TASK space is allowed to ask for. The file
# beside this one bounds the joints; this one bounds the tool point, and the two
# are different quantities rather than two views of one (ADR-0027).
#
# Only Pilz reads these, and only its LIN and CIRC generators do — PTP plans in
# joint space and never consults them. They are loaded regardless, because
# `pilz_industrial_motion_planner::CartesianLimit` is constructed when the
# pipeline initialises: the four keys are declared without defaults, and a
# missing one is an uncaught `rclcpp::ParameterUninitializedException` that takes
# move_group down at start-up rather than a planner that declines a LIN request.
#
# MoveIt reads them from `robot_description_planning.cartesian_limits.*`; the
# prefix is applied by whoever loads this file, because it is a MoveIt convention
# and not a fact about the facility. The deceleration sign is MoveIt's too, and
# is applied here for the same reason the joint one is.
#
# Three things about these four numbers that a reader should not have to
# discover, because the L0 comment beside them once implied otherwise.
#
#   * They are consumed by NO motion in this cell today. Every motion is planned
#     by a joint-space planner, which never reads them. They exist so that the
#     pipeline can be constructed at all.
#   * `max_trans_dec` is read by nothing in Jazzy's Pilz beyond one debug log:
#     the shipped `libpilz_industrial_motion_planner.so` contains the parameter
#     name and its descriptor and no other reference, and the planner's own
#     headers never mention it. It is declared because the parameter listener
#     declares all four without defaults, not because it bounds anything.
#   * A Cartesian ceiling cannot guarantee a joint stays inside its own. The
#     required joint rate for a tool velocity is `J^-1 x_dot`, which is unbounded
#     near a singularity, so for ANY positive value here there is a pose at which
#     the joint ceiling is reached first. What actually bounds the joints is
#     Pilz's per-sample joint check, not this file.
cartesian_limits:
  max_trans_vel: {{ arm.max_cartesian_velocity_m_s | f }}
  max_trans_acc: {{ arm.max_cartesian_acceleration_m_s2 | f }}
  max_trans_dec: {{ -arm.max_cartesian_deceleration_m_s2 | f }}
  max_rot_vel: {{ arm.max_cartesian_rotational_velocity_rad_s | f }}
